statement:
A C array declared `double a[N][N]`, with `N = 4096`. Two loops that do exactly the same arithmetic:

```c
/* version A */
for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)
    for (int j = 0; j < N; j++)
        sum += a[i][j];

/* version B */
for (int j = 0; j < N; j++)
    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)
        sum += a[i][j];
```

Version A runs about **eight times faster** than version B on a machine with 64-byte cache lines.

**What is the reason?**
